Vendored mailon source
======================

`skills/mail/vendor/mailon/` is vendored source from the
`orientpine/emailAutomation` repository. The source was added to this
repository by commit `ba67e73a94f8f650ac2364f51018b8c588a45dea` on
2026-07-30 (`feat(mail): vendor mailon engine into repo (integrate emailAutomation)`).

Per the repository owner's D9 attestation (2026-08-11), this vendored source
is the owner's work and is covered by the repository's Apache-2.0 license.
The vendored source files are kept byte-identical to the upstream material,
except for the local fixes listed below. `orientpine/emailAutomation` was
absorbed into this repository, so there is no separate upstream to send them
to; per the root `AGENTS.md` vendoring convention they are applied to the
vendor source directly and redeployed.

Local fixes
-----------

* 2026-08-18 — `mailon/login.py`: login completion was decided by
  `wait_url("**/mail**")`, and the URL was validated only inside that wait's
  `except` branch. The glob matches the *host* (`mailon.kr` contains "mail"),
  so the wait returned immediately, the guard never ran, and a rejected
  credential was logged as "login succeeded" — the real failure then surfaced
  much later as a `BrowserError`, which exit code 2 folds into
  `auth_or_browser_error`. Login completion is now decided by polling the URL
  *path* and is validated on every exit from the wait. Regression:
  `tests/unit/test_mailon_login_detection.py`.

* 2026-08-18 — `mailon/resolve.py`: `resolve_name()` called
  `window._tbar.compose()` as soon as login returned. Reaching `/mail` does not
  mean the mailbox SPA can service that call yet, and compose then throws inside
  the site's own `main.js`. That became a `BrowserError`, which exit code 2
  again folds into `auth_or_browser_error`, so a startup race was reported as an
  auth failure. A readiness *predicate* does not fix it: measured, the page
  passes through several distinct broken states (`_tbar` undefined, then
  `tabPanel._getMenuById is not a function`), so a guard that only asked whether
  `tabPanel` was defined passed while compose still failed. Compose is now
  driven by `open_compose_when_ready()`, which retries the real call until it
  succeeds — opening compose is read-only, nothing is submitted — and fails with
  a named timeout carrying the last error otherwise. Regression:
  `tests/unit/test_mailon_compose_readiness.py`.

* 2026-08-18 — `mailon/send.py`, `mailon/send_trigger.py`, and
  `mailon/resolve.py`: the send path still called `window._tbar.compose()`
  directly, so dry runs reached the measured mailbox SPA startup race without
  the retry already protecting recipient resolution. Real sends survived only
  incidentally because duplicate suppression delayed compose by 3–5 seconds;
  dry runs skip that block and failed after login with
  `FAIL [external_service_error]` (exit 2). The proven retry helper now lives in
  `send_trigger.py`, which owns compose/send triggering, and both resolution and
  sending share it. The 3-second form settle remains because only compose
  callability has been measured, not completion of CSRF, recipient-field, and
  editor rendering. Regression: `tests/unit/test_mailon_compose_readiness.py`.

The private development repository preserves those bytes. Public-history
snapshots run `automation/public_export_redaction.py` against the materialized
copy only, replacing identifying fixture/comment values without changing the
tracked vendor files or the private upstream provenance.

The following third-party packages are version-pinned runtime dependencies
only; their source is not vendored here:

* pyotp==2.10.0
* python-dotenv==1.2.2
* beautifulsoup4==4.15.0
* lxml==6.1.1

Transitive dependencies are also pinned in `requirements.txt` for
reproducibility. Their own licensing terms remain applicable to those
dependencies.
